<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gaspé Peninsula</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gaspé Peninsula is a peninsula in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, between the estuary of the St Lawrence and the Bay of Chaleur. In 1534 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-cartier">Jacques Cartier</a> landed at Gaspé Bay and raised a cross claiming the land for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. A homeland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikmaq">Mi'kmaq</a>, it was later settled by French, Acadian, Loyalist and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/channel-islands">Channel Islands</a> fishing communities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gaspe-peninsula.jpg" alt="Gaspé Peninsula" width="300" height="271" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GaspePeninsula23.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Jersey merchants and seamen joined the Newfoundland cod fishery, and in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries firms such as that of Charles Robin ran fishing stations on the <b>Gaspé</b> coast of Canada, linking the island to markets in Europe and the Americas, while wartime privateering brought further profits.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bailiwick-of-jersey">Bailiwick of Jersey · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>France’s claim in North America began in 1534, when Jacques Cartier, sailing for Francis I, explored the Gulf of St Lawrence and planted a cross on the <b>Gaspé Peninsula</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
